#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173198 :tutorial/content "Given a visualization, it's possible to determine characteristics about the underlying vector field.\n\nFor example, let's consider the visualization below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e3ecb8b2d64aef1cf4654fea8e3ad8f176e9d830ee8b402a281155c3b4ef645.png)\n\nThis visualization was generated by one of the following functions.\n\n1. $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf H(x,y) = y \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\nWe can determine which function generated the above diagram using a process of elimination:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. This means we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf j$ component is negative for some $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}$$\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. This means that the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is dependent on the variable $x.$\n\nSo, we can exclude any function whose $\\mathbf i$ component is independent of $x.$ Hence, we can rule out the following option: $$\\mathbf H(x,y) = y\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf G(x,y) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "372dc560-67d1-5888-9353-8f8f365cff15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5773 :tutorial/title "Analyzing Vector Field Diagrams"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173252 :tutorial/content "Consider the surface integral of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2\n$$\n\n \nover the parametric surface $S,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: 2v, \\: -u \\big\\rangle, \\quad (u,v)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is the unit square in the $uv$-plane given by\n\n$$\nD = \\{(u,v) \\: | \\: 0\\leq u\\leq 1,\\: 0\\leq v\\leq 1\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the surface integral of $f$ over $S.$\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the surface integral of $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2,\\,0,\\,2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=2v\\,z=-u} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+(2v)^{2}+(-u)^{2} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(2{u}^{2}+4{v}^{2})\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}2{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}+8\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v+8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5620ba9b-3795-5fdb-bc39-1699fb66c6b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7623 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173256 :tutorial/content "Consider a continuous scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ and a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ defined for $(u,v) \\in D,$ where $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ \n\nIf our surface is \"sufficiently smooth,\" we can define the **surface integral** of $f$ over $S.$ Let's discuss how we might construct such an integral.\n\nFor the sake of simplicity, let's assume that our region $D$ is rectangular. We start by creating a partition $P$ of $D$ in the usual way:\n\n$$\nP = \\left\\{u_0, u_1, \\ldots, u_m\\right\\} \\times \\left\\{v_0, v_1, \\ldots, v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nOur partition is shown schematically below.\n\n![](images/6a/6a15c7b587503c82a57d0734c5bc5fda990824ad162724236868461cd92e4fbe.png)\n\n \n\nThe area $\\delta A = \\delta u\\delta v$ of the small rectangle highlighted in the diagram corresponds to a particular patch $S_{ij}$ of area $\\delta S_{ij}$ on our surface. \n\n![](images/1a/1a8774b7aa366d3328b6f81cfe7315b7784a1f5783f14c1f19f83a459cef5434.png)\n\n \n\nLet $P_{ij}^*$ be a point on the surface within the patch $S_{ij}.$ We consider the product \n\n$$\nf(P_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nIf we compute this product for all patches and add them together, we get the Riemann sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n f(P_{ij}^*)\\cdot\\delta S_{ij}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $\\delta u, \\delta v\\to 0$ gives our definition of the surface integral of $f$ over $S,$ which we denote as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFrom previous discussions, we know that the area of the small patch can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\delta S_{ij} \\approx \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta u\\delta v = \\| \\mathbf r'_u \\times \\mathbf r'_v\\|\\,\\delta A\n$$\n\nIt follows that we can calculate our surface integral using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThis formula is also valid when $D$ is non-rectangular. \n\nLet's see an example of applying this in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "31cee43f-e940-5add-90d5-831ffcaaafc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179482} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179645 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179483} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for Flux"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179484} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179485} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Rectangle Using Two Symmetry Properties"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179524} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179525} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11fb303b-053a-56d1-9b96-acd75c748b51" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179529} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179530} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f04a969-e7e0-592f-b5cd-e92fedb217a8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Cartesian Curves"}]}
